 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Wavelet-based Adaptive Resolution Volume Rendering](1171-wavelet-based-adaptive-resolution-volume-rendering.md)
-A parallel rendering technique that reduces reconstruction cost by storing reconstructed data at pre-selected wavelet tree nodes. It traverses the wavelet tree according to a user-specified error tolerance to identify and decompress data blocks of varying resolutions, which are then rendered in parallel. It sets itself apart by optimizing for parallel load balancing and minimizing run-time communication costs through hierarchical space-filling curve distribution.
+A parallel rendering technique that reduces reconstruction cost by storing reconstructed data at pre-selected wavelet tree nodes based on available storage resources. It traverses the wavelet tree according to a user-specified error tolerance to identify and decompress data blocks of varying resolutions, which are then rendered in parallel to compose the final image. It sets itself apart from its siblings by explicitly optimizing for parallel load balancing and minimizing run-time communication costs through hierarchical space-filling curve distribution and error-guided bucketization, rather than focusing solely on single-processor memory hierarchy or splatting optimization.
 
 ### [Wavelet-based Multiresolution Rendering](1588-wavelet-based-multiresolution-rendering.md)
-An interactive rendering method for isosurfaces that represents the underlying trivariate function as a spline wavelet hierarchy. It enables adaptive, view-dependent selection of level-of-detail by superimposing appropriately weighted basis functions. It distinguishes itself by combining GPU-accelerated wavelet reconstruction with a view-dependent error metric to selectively activate octree chunks.
+An interactive rendering method for isosurfaces in a voxel grid that represents the underlying trivariate function as a spline wavelet hierarchy. It enables adaptive, view-dependent selection of level-of-detail by superimposing appropriately weighted basis functions, allowing distant parts of the volume to be rendered at lower resolution to improve performance with minimal visual impact. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods by explicitly combining GPU-accelerated wavelet reconstruction with a view-dependent error metric to selectively activate octree chunks, ensuring continuity by reconstructing necessary neighbors.
 
 ### [Hierarchical Wavelet Transform on Omnitrees](2216-hierarchical-wavelet-transform-on-omnitrees.md)
-A compression strategy that transforms nodal omnitree data into wavelet coefficients to enable lossless or lossy compression via pruning and tree restructuring. It solves the problem of minimizing storage size for 3D volumetric data by exploiting anisotropic refinement, setting itself apart from isotropic siblings by explicitly defining mathematical basis functions over the omnitree structure.
+A compression strategy that transforms nodal omnitree data into wavelet coefficients to enable lossless or lossy compression via pruning and tree restructuring (downsplit). It solves the problem of minimizing storage size for 3D volumetric data by exploiting anisotropic refinement, setting itself apart from isotropic siblings like [Wavelet-based Multiresolution Rendering](1588-wavelet-based-multiresolution-rendering.md) by explicitly defining mathematical basis functions over the omnitree structure to achieve superior approximation rates.
 
 ### [L2-optimal wavelet splatting](2816-l2-optimal-wavelet-splatting.md)
-A volume rendering technique that decomposes volume data into a wavelet pyramid representation in the spline domain and computes splats via an orthogonal projection operator using least-squares approximation. It sets itself apart by ensuring the smallest possible approximation error for a given sampling step size and adapting the grid resolution to the basis function size.
+A volume rendering technique that decomposes volume data into a wavelet pyramid representation in the spline domain and computes splats via an orthogonal projection operator using least-squares approximation on an adaptive multiresolution grid. It sets itself apart from standard interpolation-based wavelet splatting (such as [Wavelet-based Multiresolution Rendering](1588-wavelet-based-multiresolution-rendering.md)) by ensuring the smallest possible approximation error for a given sampling step size and by adapting the grid resolution to the basis function size, which yields significant performance gains at coarse scales.
 
 ### [Point-wise Inverse Discrete Wavelet Transform (PW-IDWT) via Generalized Projection](3394-point-wise-inverse-discrete-wavelet-transform-pw-idwt-via-ge.md)
-Reconstructs a single signal sample from a wavelet decomposition by determining the minimal set of subband coefficients required. It solves the problem of identifying which coefficients at a coarser level contribute to a specific sample at a finer level, exploiting the recursive nature of the lifting scheme. Unlike methods that decode entire subbands, this approach calculates the exact Generalized Projection of a sample into the transformed domain.
+The Point-wise Inverse Discrete Wavelet Transform (PW-IDWT) via Generalized Projection (GP) reconstructs a single signal sample (or a set of samples) from a wavelet decomposition by determining the minimal set of subband coefficients required. It solves the problem of identifying which coefficients at a coarser level contribute to a specific sample at a finer level during synthesis, exploiting the recursive nature of the lifting scheme. Unlike methods that decode entire subbands, this approach calculates the exact Generalized Projection of a sample into the transformed domain, minimizing the number of subband images to decode. It builds on [Wavelet-based Volume Rendering](1052-wavelet-based-volume-rendering.md) by providing an efficient, sample-specific inverse transform mechanism [G].
 
 ### Block-wise Wavelet Reconstruction
-A reconstruction method that processes wavelet coefficients in blocks rather than point-wise to reduce redundant computation [S4, unofficial]. It reconstructs a block of voxels along the z dimension by convolving the input with a wavelet kernel of size $m$ row by row, performing three passes (one for each dimension) [S4, unofficial]. The computational cost for one pass is $mn^3$, where $m$ is the kernel size and $n$ is the dimension size, totaling $3mn^3$ for three passes, which is more efficient than point-wise cost $m^3n^3$ by a factor of $k = m^2/3$ [S4, unofficial]. Pitfall: Adjacent blocks have redundant computation because the wavelet kernel extends outside the target block boundary [S4, unofficial].
+A reconstruction method that processes wavelet coefficients in blocks rather than point-wise to reduce redundant computation during volume rendering [S4, unofficial]. It reconstructs a block of voxels along the z dimension by convolving the input with a wavelet kernel of size $m$ row by row, performing three passes (one for each dimension) to complete the reconstruction [S4, unofficial]. The computational cost for one pass is $mn^3$, where $m$ is the kernel size and $n$ is the dimension size. Total cost for three passes is $3mn^3$. Point-wise cost is $m^3n^3$. The efficiency factor is $k = m^2/3$ [S4, unofficial]. For Haar wavelet, $m=2$; for Daubechies W6, $m=6$ [S4, unofficial]. A pitfall is that adjacent blocks have redundant computation because the wavelet kernel extends outside the target block boundary by approximately half the kernel length [S4, unofficial].
 
 ### On-the-fly Voxel Reconstruction
-A method to reconstruct voxel values on-the-fly from wavelet coefficients during rendering, avoiding pre-computation of the entire volume [S4, unofficial]. Given a target voxel $(x_o, y_o, z_o)$ and a wavelet coefficient at level $k$, it maps indices to level $k$ by dividing by $2^k$, finds corresponding voxels in the other seven regions of the decomposed data, and applies the inverse wavelet transformation iteratively down to level 0 [S4, unofficial].
+A method to reconstruct voxel values on-the-fly from wavelet coefficients during rendering, avoiding pre-computation of the entire volume [S4, unofficial]. Given a wavelet coefficient with $k$ levels and a target voxel $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$, it finds the voxel from level $k$ by mapping indices $x_k, y_k, z_k$ by dividing $x_0, y_0, z_0$ by $2^k$ [S4, unofficial]. It finds the corresponding voxel in the other seven regions of the decomposed data (fHHH, fLLH, etc.) by adding half the size of the level $k$ dimensions to the indices [S4, unofficial]. It applies the inverse wavelet transformation to these eight sub-cubes to reconstruct a new sub-cube with size doubled in each axis, repeating until level 0 is reached [S4, unofficial].
 
